# Flink Streaming API Basics

## Overview
Apache Flink is a distributed stream processing framework. This skill covers essential concepts and patterns for building streaming applications.

## Core Concepts

### 1. StreamExecutionEnvironment
The entry point for creating streaming applications.

```java
StreamExecutionEnvironment env = StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();
```

### 2. Reading Data from Sources
Flink can read from various sources. For file-based sources, use `readTextFile()` or custom `SourceFunction`.

```java
// Simple text file source
DataStream<String> text = env.readTextFile("path/to/file.txt");

// Custom source using SourceFunction
DataStream<MyEvent> events = env.addSource(new MyCustomSource());
```

### 3. Transformations
Key transformations for stream processing:

#### Map - Transform each element
```java
DataStream<Integer> mapped = stream.map(x -> x * 2);
```

#### FlatMap - Emit zero or more elements
```java
stream.flatMap((value, out) -> {
    if (value > 0) out.collect(value);
})
```

#### Filter - Keep only matching elements
```java
DataStream<Integer> filtered = stream.filter(x -> x > 10);
```

#### KeyBy - Group elements by key
```java
DataStream<Event> keyed = stream.keyBy(Event::getJobId);
```

### 4. Window Operations
Windowing is crucial for stateful operations on streams.

```java
// Tumbling window of 10 seconds
stream.keyBy(...)
    .window(TumblingProcessingTimeWindows.of(Time.seconds(10)))
    .apply(...)

// Session window - triggers on inactivity
stream.keyBy(...)
    .window(ProcessingTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.seconds(600)))
    .apply(...)
```

### 5. Process Function (Advanced State Management)
For complex stateful operations:

```java
stream.keyBy(...)
    .process(new KeyedProcessFunction<K, V, R>() {
        @Override
        public void processElement(V value, Context ctx, Collector<R> out) {
            // Custom processing logic
        }
    })
```

### 6. Sinks (Writing Output)
Output the results to a destination:

```java
// Write to file
stream.addSink(new StreamingFileSink.BulkFormatBuilder<>(
    new Path("output/path"),
    new SimpleStringEncoder<>()
).build());

// Custom sink
stream.addSink(new SinkFunction<String>() {
    @Override
    public void invoke(String value, Context context) {
        // Write to destination
    }
});
```

## Common Patterns

### Grouping and Aggregation
```java
stream.keyBy(Event::getJobId)
    .process(new KeyedProcessFunction<String, Event, Result>() {
        private transient MapState<String, Integer> taskCounts;

        @Override
        public void open(Configuration parameters) {
            MapStateDescriptor<String, Integer> desc =
                new MapStateDescriptor<>("taskCounts", String.class, Integer.class);
            taskCounts = getRuntimeContext().getMapState(desc);
        }

        @Override
        public void processElement(Event e, Context ctx, Collector<Result> out) {
            // Process with state
        }
    })
```

### Joining Streams
```java
stream1.join(stream2)
    .where(e1 -> e1.getKey())
    .equalTo(e2 -> e2.getKey())
    .window(TumblingProcessingTimeWindows.of(Time.seconds(5)))
    .apply((first, second) -> {
        // Join logic
    })
```

## References
- [Flink Documentation: DataStream API](https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-release-1.18/docs/dev/datastream/overview/)
- [Flink Windows](https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-release-1.18/docs/dev/datastream/operators/windows/)
- [ProcessFunction](https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-release-1.18/docs/dev/datastream/operators/process_function/)

# Processing Gzipped CSV Files in Flink

## Overview
Working with compressed CSV files (gzip format) in Flink streaming applications requires custom source functions.

## Reading Gzipped CSV Files

### Custom SourceFunction Implementation

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.source.SourceFunction;
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.util.zip.GZIPInputStream;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class GZIPCsvSource implements SourceFunction<String> {
    private String filePath;
    private volatile boolean isRunning = true;

    public GZIPCsvSource(String filePath) {
        this.filePath = filePath;
    }

    @Override
    public void run(SourceContext<String> ctx) throws Exception {
        try (GZIPInputStream gzip = new GZIPInputStream(
                Files.newInputStream(Paths.get(filePath)));
             BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(
                new InputStreamReader(gzip))) {

            String line;
            while (isRunning && (line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                ctx.collect(line);
            }
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel() {
        isRunning = false;
    }
}
```

### Usage in Job

```java
DataStream<String> csvLines = env.addSource(
    new GZIPCsvSource("/path/to/file.csv.gz")
);

// Parse CSV lines into objects
DataStream<MyEvent> events = csvLines
    .filter(line -> !line.isEmpty())
    .map(line -> parseEvent(line));
```

## Parsing CSV Records

### String to Object Mapping

```java
private static MyEvent parseEvent(String line) {
    String[] fields = line.split(",");
    return new MyEvent(
        Long.parseLong(fields[0]),    // timestamp
        Long.parseLong(fields[1]),    // job id
        Integer.parseInt(fields[2]),  // event type
        fields[3]                     // other field
    );
}
```

### Handling Missing/Empty Fields

```java
private static Integer parseIntField(String field) {
    if (field == null || field.isEmpty()) {
        return null;  // or use a default value
    }
    try {
        return Integer.parseInt(field);
    } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
        return null;
    }
}
```

## Timestamp Extraction

For events with timestamps (often in microseconds in cluster traces):

```java
// If using event time (recommended for accuracy)
DataStream<MyEvent> withTimestamps = events
    .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(
        WatermarkStrategy
            .forMonotonousTimestamps()
            .withTimestampAssigner((event, recordTimestamp) ->
                event.getTimestampMicros() / 1000  // Convert to milliseconds
            )
    );
```

## Key Considerations

1. **Blocking Operation**: SourceFunction runs synchronously. For large files, this is acceptable but consider parallelization.

2. **Resource Management**: Always use try-with-resources for proper cleanup of streams and readers.

3. **Timestamps**: Cluster data often uses microsecond precision. Convert to Flink's millisecond precision appropriately.

4. **Error Handling**: Skip malformed lines gracefully or log them for debugging.

## References
- [Flink SourceFunction Documentation](https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-release-1.18/docs/dev/datastream/sources/custom_source/)
- [Java GZIPInputStream](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/11/docs/api/java.base/java/util/zip/GZIPInputStream.html)

# Stateful Stream Processing in Flink

## Overview
Stateful processing in Flink maintains information across multiple events in a stream, essential for tracking aggregations, sessions, and complex business logic.

## Key State Concepts

### 1. Keyed State
State associated with a specific key, only accessible after keyBy().

```java
stream.keyBy(Event::getJobId)
    .process(new KeyedProcessFunction<String, Event, Result>() {
        private transient ValueState<String> lastState;

        @Override
        public void open(Configuration parameters) {
            // Initialize state descriptor
            ValueStateDescriptor<String> descriptor =
                new ValueStateDescriptor<>("lastEvent", String.class);
            lastState = getRuntimeContext().getState(descriptor);
        }

        @Override
        public void processElement(Event event, Context ctx, Collector<Result> out) {
            String previous = lastState.value();
            lastState.update(event.getValue());
            // Process with state
        }
    });
```

### 2. State Types

#### ValueState - Single value per key
```java
ValueStateDescriptor<Integer> descriptor =
    new ValueStateDescriptor<>("count", Integer.class);
ValueState<Integer> count = getRuntimeContext().getState(descriptor);

int current = count.value() != null ? count.value() : 0;
count.update(current + 1);
```

#### MapState - Key-value mappings per key
```java
MapStateDescriptor<String, Integer> descriptor =
    new MapStateDescriptor<>("map", String.class, Integer.class);
MapState<String, Integer> map = getRuntimeContext().getMapState(descriptor);

map.put("key1", 42);
Integer value = map.get("key1");
map.remove("key1");
Iterable<String> keys = map.keys();
```

#### ListState - List of values per key
```java
ListStateDescriptor<Event> descriptor =
    new ListStateDescriptor<>("events", Event.class);
ListState<Event> list = getRuntimeContext().getListState(descriptor);

list.add(newEvent);
for (Event e : list.get()) {
    // Process each event
}
list.clear();
```

#### AggregatingState - Aggregate values
```java
AggregatingStateDescriptor<Event, Long, Long> descriptor =
    new AggregatingStateDescriptor<>(
        "sum",
        new AggregateFunction<Event, Long, Long>() {
            @Override
            public Long createAccumulator() { return 0L; }

            @Override
            public Long add(Event event, Long acc) {
                return acc + event.getValue();
            }

            @Override
            public Long getResult(Long acc) { return acc; }

            @Override
            public Long merge(Long acc1, Long acc2) {
                return acc1 + acc2;
            }
        },
        Long.class
    );
```

## Timers

Essential for triggering state-based actions after a timeout.

```java
@Override
public void processElement(Event event, Context ctx, Collector<Result> out) {
    // Register a timer for 10 minutes in the future (event time)
    long timerTime = event.getTimestamp() + 10 * 60 * 1000;
    ctx.timerService().registerEventTimeTimer(timerTime);
}

@Override
public void onTimer(long timestamp, OnTimerContext ctx, Collector<Result> out) {
    // Timer fired - check state and emit results
    String jobId = ctx.getCurrentKey();
    MyState state = stateVariable.value();
    // Emit results based on state
}
```

### Time Semantics

- **Event Time**: Timestamp within the data (most accurate for analysis)
- **Processing Time**: System clock when processed (simpler, less accurate)
- **Ingestion Time**: Time when data entered Flink

```java
// Using event time (recommended for bounded data)
WatermarkStrategy<Event> strategy = WatermarkStrategy
    .<Event>forMonotonousTimestamps()
    .withTimestampAssigner((event, recordTimestamp) ->
        event.getTimestamp()
    );

DataStream<Event> timestamped = events
    .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(strategy);
```

## Session Windows Pattern

For detecting inactivity periods and grouping events:

```java
stream.keyBy(Event::getJobId)
    .window(ProcessingTimeSessionWindows.withGap(
        Time.minutes(10)
    ))
    .apply(new WindowFunction<Event, Result, String, TimeWindow>() {
        @Override
        public void apply(String jobId, TimeWindow window,
                         Iterable<Event> events, Collector<Result> out) {
            int count = 0;
            for (Event e : events) {
                count++;
            }
            out.collect(new Result(jobId, count));
        }
    });
```

## State Cleanup

Important to prevent unbounded state growth:

```java
@Override
public void processElement(Event event, Context ctx, Collector<Result> out) {
    // ... process ...

    // Clear old state after processing
    long cleanupTime = event.getTimestamp() + 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
    ctx.timerService().registerEventTimeTimer(cleanupTime);
}

@Override
public void onTimer(long timestamp, OnTimerContext ctx, Collector<Result> out) {
    // Clear state on timer
    stateVariable.clear();
}
```

## References
- [Flink State Documentation](https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-release-1.18/docs/dev/datastream/fault_tolerance/state/)
- [Flink Timers](https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-release-1.18/docs/dev/datastream/operators/process_function/#timers)
- [Working with State](https://nightlies.apache.org/flink/flink-docs-release-1.18/docs/dev/datastream/fault_tolerance/working_with_state/)

